WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.680
This is the Technical Difficulties.
We're playing Citation Needed.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.720
Joining me today:
He reads books, you know — it's Chris Joel!

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.520
Follow me on Twitter at toast @...!

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.740
No, it's bollocks.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:18.940
[Laughter]

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.940
Everybody's favourite Gary Brannan — Gary Brannan!

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.340
[Sings]  'Pull down your pants and s*** on the ants,
In an English country garden.'

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.000
I'm going to have to get the bleep out in the first minute!

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.700
Thought I'd make it easy for you.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.720
And the bounciest man on stage — Matt Gray!

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.360
We're live! We can do visual gags!

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:44.860
[Laughter]

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:48.700
[Laughter]

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.080
[Laughter]

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.620
Like Bob Dylan before he went electric.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.120
In front of me I've got an article from Wikipedia
and these folks can't see it.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.340
Every fact they get right is a point and a ding [DING],

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.860
And there's a special prize for
particularly good answers, which is...

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.600
[Audience sings]

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.840
MATT and TOM: Oh yeah.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.880
And today we are talking about Ruth Belville.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.040
MATT: Belville?

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.940
GARY: Belville! A town of bells!

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.920
TOM: I mean, I don't imagine that's the etymology...

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.820
Well, you never know, do you?

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.440
Oh! Is it a town of Ruth Bells?

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:22.300
Oh!

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.640
Ruth Bell-ville. Right.
Okay, sorry. That took me a second.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.220
No. Two words, one name.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.740
Is it a person or a place?

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.000
TOM: It is definitely a person.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.080
MATT: It's a person?
GARY: It's a person.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.160
CHRIS: Is it a woman?

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.540
TOM: Yes.
GARY: Get in!

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:36.220
[DING]

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:37.500
Point for that man!

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.320
MATT: First point of the day.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.160
Is anyone keeping track of the points here?
Because I'm not. I'll be honest.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:42.960
[Laughter]

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.260
GARY: Well, we never do.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.960
TOM: Do you want to guess
whenabouts we're talking about?

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.360
The Victorian era!

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.300
[DING]

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.580
GARY: When we play our best.
CHRIS: Never fails.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.180
GARY: The time of big hats and iron!

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.700
Does she have any iron?

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.080
[Laughter]

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:01.880
Careful answer!

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.400
She has some silver.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.920
Used to be gold, it's now silver.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.580
So she has no iron.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.180
She's not massively anaemic,
if that's what you're saying.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:11.120
That's not in here.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.820
Yeah, you know me, Tom.
I'm right there on the anaemia gags.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.440
Did she have a trouser press?

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.380
TOM: She is famous for having a physical object.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.460
Oh great. So we've got a woman who has a thing.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.420
With silver, that was originally gold...

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.080
GARY: A silver thing.
MATT: Was it a goblet?

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.040
TOM: I'm sorry, was it a what?

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.360
Was it a goblet?

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:29.700
CHRIS: Hang on, a gold...?

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.820
That's not the gesture for 'goblet', Matt.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.520
MATT: One can cup a cup.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.700
Yeah!

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.340
TOM: We've found the audience's level. That's good.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.580
And it's down there!

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.400
It is something that would come in silver and gold.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.940
It would have a silver or gold case on it.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:50.760
MATT: Was it a jewellery?

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:51.600
Watch!

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.320
TOM: Point! [DING]
MATT and GARY: Wahey!

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.280
TOM: You're absolutely right.
MATT: You're our points man.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.340
Absolutely.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.760
It's the beard that's feared.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.080
Elizabeth Ruth Naomi Belville, 1854 to 1943,

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.640
and known for carrying around a watch.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:05.600
Okay.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.380
Was she... I'm going to go with something answery.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.680
Something to do with trains and time zones?

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.860
I mean, given it's a watch — it's to do with time.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.040
[Laughter]

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:17.460
No!

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:18.860
MATT: I'm not hearing a ding!
[DING]

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:19.980
CHRIS: Official keeper of the Queen's O'Clock.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.780
Oh, you know what... [DING]

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.900
It's not quite — it's close. Official Keeper of
the Queen's O'Clock... we're getting closer.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.520
CHRIS: Corgi s***ting time.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.340
Greenwich Mean Time
certainly has something to do with it.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.080
Corgi S***ting Time...

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.480
I've just realized I said that.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.040
It's little used. It's 37 minutes off the hour.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.160
MATT: CST.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.220
'Excuse me, Ma'am, I just need to take the corgis.'

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.840
[Queen voice] 'Oh yes, it's s***ting time.'

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.180
Just going to set my watch to Corgi S***ting Time.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.940
I tell you what, it is basically —
that 37 minutes is corgi s***ting time.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.960
'Finchley! They're still in the parlour!'

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.700
Someone at Buckingham Palace somewhere is
pulling out the elaborate silver dish right now.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:58.920
CHRIS: Used to be gold, you know.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.360
It does tarnish so.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.280
GARY: Yes!

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:12.040
Why would someone in Victorian London,
and later, up to World War Two,

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.080
be going around with a watch?

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:17.220
Was she an official clock-setter
for some town or borough?

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.060
TOM: Hmm... I'll give you a point for that. [DING]

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:20.520
Certainly — I don't think official. This was not...

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.100
Unofficial. Going around setting it
to her own bloody time.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.920
She's a guerrilla time setter.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.380
GARY: You can't set time on a gorilla!

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.760
They don't wear watches!

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.560
It's like a s*** Banksy!

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:36.460
'Ah ha ha! You thought it was
five minutes later than it is!'

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.260
GARY: Yeah, but would —
MATT: 'But I *know* it's wrong, 'cos I have a watch!'

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.200
Ah! Now...

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.800
Can I make the point, if the gorilla told you the time,
you wouldn't disagree with it though, would you?

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.420
Whatever time he says it is.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.460
TOM: I will point out, you're getting very close there.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.080
This is how...
[Laughter]

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.080
No, Gary isn't. Matt is.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.880
This is someone who went round selling...

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.700
MATT: Oh! Oh!
CHRIS: Time.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:04.220
'I know the time! Give me a fiver
and I'll tell you the time' — kind of person.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:05.020
[DING]
TOM: Yup.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:05.820
Oh my God.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.200
TOM: Absolutely right. Spot on.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.080
I bet you can do that in Leicester Square.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.660
I mean, you wouldn't want to do it in Parliament Square,
because you've got Big Ben there,

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.780
but Leicester Square...

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:14.780
[Laughter]

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.080
GARY: 'It's 11:55.'
DONGGG...

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.700
'Trust me. That's wrong.'

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:25.380
That's not actually Big Ben.
That's just Gary going 'DONGGG...'

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.860
Which bit of me, remains open to be seen.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.320
If you do go to Parliament Square on a Thursday night,

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.280
you can just see him standing in the corner
doing an impression of Big Ben.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.720
Yeah! I even put the pointy hat on and everything.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.040
And for some tourists, the very small / far away thing,
it does work as well.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:42.720
How big's the pendulum, Gary?

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:43.740
You don't want to know.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:44.400
Hahey!

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.600
All I'll tell you is, it's regulated by a penny every year.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.660
That's true, innit? They regulate
the pendulum on the clock on Big Ben

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.500
by putting pennies on or subtracting it,
to half a second or something?

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:55.740
Something like that, yeah.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:56.680
Old pennies.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.420
Well, with the exchange rate at the moment,
it's going to be pounds by the end of the year.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:05:59.400
We're rich!

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.800
And you wanted us to do the Brexit one. There you go.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.960
Yes. This was a woman called Ruth Belville,
who like her father and her father before her...

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.800
Her father and *his* father before *him*...
[Laughter]

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.360
GARY: Get a diagram!

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:15.480
MATT: For generations.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.660
Pronouns are always difficult.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:18.220
MATT: Generations. You need the word 'generations'.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:23.580
For generations, had sold the time to people
who needed to know it accurately.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:25.580
So where did she go to get that?

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:26.700
Time Magazine.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.140
It's a grudging Biscuits.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.500
But it's a Biscuits.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.020
Oh, we're not putting scales of biscuitry in here, are we?

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.820
So where did she go to get the proper time
in Victorian London?

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:52.340
This was originally 1836 when her father created it.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.360
The London Time Dump.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.140
[Laughter]
TOM: Now...

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:59.000
That's a good description for what this would be.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.380
GARY: Oh great! I win then.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:02.960
I'm not giving you a point for it...

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.020
but where in London —

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:05.540
GARY: Damn my short arms!

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:07.200
Where would you go to get the time?

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.280
Greenwich, I would assume.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:09.160
Yeah, Greenwich.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.460
Yep. [DING] Absolutely right. Greenwich Observatory.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:12.600
You see the nonchalance on that?
[Click]  Yeah, it's fine.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.260
Greenwich Observatory, you would go
to the clock that is outside it...

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:18.140
Surely you'd go to its herb garden.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:20.740
-- [Groans] Thyme!
-- Oh, f*** you.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:21.760
Oh.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:29.560
I heard a muttering of discontent in the crowd there.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.880
GARY: That got the reaction
it purely deserved, which was 'Ughh...'

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.720
That's just the despair of 400 people in front of me.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.720
That is what my puns are worth!

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:39.360
Ahh.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.180
That's bat under the arm, back into the pavilion,
shaking your head — really, I'm sorry.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:47.560
Yes. There is a clock outside
called the Shephard Gate Clock...

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:48.880
What's unusual about it?

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:49.940
MATT: It's got sheep on it!

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:50.900
No hands on it.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:52.820
Shepherds only can go under it!

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.860
No to all of those things!

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:57.700
Are you sure?

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.500
I mean, it's set on a wall, so if anyone
could get under it I'd be very surprised.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.080
Is it on a Viking street named Shepherd Street?

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.920
Er, no. It's by the Shephard Gate of the Observatory.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.560
Sheep work like sundials, don't they?

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.480
They all face the same way...

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.160
TOM: Go on...?

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.680
Yeah, 'cos when you go past many fine Saxon churches,

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:26.940
what you find engraved on it is four holes
for you to hook the hooves in.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.100
For a sheep.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.780
It's twenty past Flossy.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:32.180
That kind of thing.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.980
Then they fall off, you get a goat,
causes all kinds of problems.

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.500
They have to be recalibrated with
a penny on their back every few rounds,

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.260
just to get them to within a few seconds.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:41.760
MATT: So I was wrong.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:42.960
TOM: Yes, you were.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.200
It is a fairly standard clock.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.520
There is something unusual about it,
particularly for that era,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.420
that you would need to tell the time, day, night...

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.360
24-hour?

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:52.400
24-hour! Have a point. [DING]

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:53.600
Absolutely right.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.700
24-hour clock, so you would go there —
or she would go there,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:02.100
would set her watch exactly, and then
go around to hundreds of clients...

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:05.560
Why didn't someone undercut her and just charge slightly less with a different watch?

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.840
Ah! And there you have stumbled upon something,
so I'll give you a point. [DING]

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.240
Oh, lucky me!

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:18.380
She came 'under attack from St John Wynne,
a director of the Standard Time Company.'

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.040
Standard Time!

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.340
'Twenty past three! Get out!'

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:29.100
What kind of criticism did he give
in a speech at the United Wards Club?

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:32.100
'Oh! I will not take time from a woman!'

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:33.860
'This is not a place for her!'
Something horrific like that?

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:35.540
You're close enough. [DING]

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:38.180
He said 'she might have been using her femininity...'

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:38.840
Eh?

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:40.820
TOM: Fe-mi-ni-ni-ty...
CHRIS: ...ni-ni-nin...

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:41.880
ALL: Femininininin...

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:44.140
'... to gain business.'

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:45.440
Oh, I see.

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:47.120
'D'you want to buy *my* time, luvvy?'

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:48.700
'Free some time?'

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.880
The speech was published in The Times...

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:53.860
Ayyyy!

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:54.800
[Applause]

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:04.240
With both of you doing that impression,
because I've got a very vivid imagination,

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.100
I've just got you in low-cut Victorian dresses
hanging round a pub in the East End...

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.760
and really I can't sleep for a fortni... oh no...

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:13.580
Hiya, luv...

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.040
Why are my hands still doing this?!

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:20.740
Augh!

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.300
So yes. Following the publication
of the comments, what happened?

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.500
She got really pissed off and threw her watch at him.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.300
But it was on a chain, so she could keep doing it.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.120
MATT: Is that like a yo-yo?

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:39.040
'Don't! Criticize! My! Timekeeping!'

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:41.000
I hope it had a bell in it,
so every time it hit him, it was like,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:42.200
Ding! Ding! Ding!

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.740
I just like the idea of,
'What time is it?'

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.460
'I've got no idea...'

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.460
'You want to know what time it is? It's whuppin' time!'

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:51.500
Er, quite the opposite, actually.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.160
MATT: Nothing.
GARY: She took it in good humour.

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:56.780
She did. It 'resulted in an increase in sales'. And, er...

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:58.240
[Laughter]

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:01.360
What did she say about his comments?

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:02.400
'T***.'

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:08.800
There was a little bit... a few more words in there —
Don't. Don't.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.240
He'd just given her free advertising.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.060
GARY: That's nails! Love that. Yeah.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:23.180
'Er, excuse me, Miss.
I hear you provide... services of time...'

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.280
'I'd like to buy some of your... time.'

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:28.640
'All right. It's 2:30. There you go.'

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:31.380
'That wasn't quite what I had in mind.'

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.860
'But my watch is accurate now, so...'

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.640
'I can't say I lost from this transaction. Good day!'

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.800
Yes. She lived to the age of 89...

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:43.260
And she'd know that, 'cos she's got a watch.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:48.980
She... how long did she have
before that for her retirement?

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.680
None at all. Because she'd just
keep going till she carked.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:53.000
Close. But a little bit.

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:54.740
Twelve minutes exactly.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:56.360
They found a watch on the body.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:58.440
GARY: Till her w...

00:11:59.000 --> 00:11:59.940
CHRIS: Come on then.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:01.680
GARY: Matt Gray's dead. Here we go.

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:04.280
TOM: That's the look that means
this is about to hit the cutting room floor.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:06.280
Everybody, roll up your sleeves. This one's coming.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:07.980
[Rising]  Ohhhh...!

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:10.040
GO!

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.260
This is terrible!

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.280
Did she meet her untimely death at...?

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:17.120
[Groans]
[Cheering]

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:18.480
Oh, no! No!

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:26.120
That wasn't even the joke!

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:31.120
I hadn't noticed that! That was better!

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.700
I was going to say, did she die at the third stroke?

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:38.720
[Groans]

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:42.540
No, but she got three years of retirement.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.800
GARY: Well, 1940 is Blitz.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.460
I think that's the start of the Blitz.
So she's walking through Blitz-torn London,

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:50.200
telling the Nazzies to eff off while she's
walking around with a clock in her hand.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:51.420
Hero!

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:54.500
Actually, yeah. 'It's teatime,
they'll be bombing in 15 minutes.'

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:56.940
How did she know that?!

00:12:57.700 --> 00:12:58.580
Ohhh!

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:01.240
Just putting it out there.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:05.180
There is a reference to a buggy in here as well,
so I imagine there was something else...

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:06.740
She may have been riding...

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:07.940
GARY: She was on a go-kart?

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:10.600
TOM: 19th — a Victorian-era go-kart?

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.740
Oh come on, Gary. It's an iron scooter!

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:13.660
Ohhh.

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:16.000
Designed by Brunel himself!

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:19.260
And there...! First reference to Brunel... 20 minutes.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:23.460
If you've got your bingo cards,
that must be a line by now, it really must.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:26.080
Says the man in a Bob's Full House hoodie.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:27.620
TOM: A reference which precisely thr...

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:30.080
TOM: ...one person in the audience —
thank you for that cheer — gets.

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:33.120
That's exactly the one in 400 people
this is aimed to appeal to.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:35.960
As am I, let's face it.

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:39.540
So, when she retired...

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:42.000
after the generations of doing this,

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.440
and it was clear that this was not necessary any more,

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:49.100
because the Standard Time Company,
while they did not win, themselves,

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:53.300
technology moved on.
What was the Standard Time Company selling?

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:57.640
CHRIS: Substandard, shoddy time. Not collected
fresh from Greenwich every morning!

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:01.160
'Just send 'em yesterday's time.
It'll be fine, it's only slightly stale.'

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.200
GARY: Big buckets of time, lined up by the gates.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.760
Filling it from the hand-pump of chronology!

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:08.140
CHRIS: 'There ye go.'

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:10.420
CHRIS: 'We've got some time back in the Devonian.'

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:11.580
CHRIS: Geology joke there...

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.320
GARY: I imagine time being dispensed sounds like,
[Blows raspberry]

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:17.080
Gary, that's how you imagine most noises.

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:18.540
Fair cop.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:21.480
Actually, we may as well,
while we're doing bingo and Gary noises...

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:22.260
Goose!

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:23.340
[Honks]

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:24.520
Goose on a train!

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:26.180
[Two-tone train horn]
[Goose honk]

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:28.380
Goose being hit by a train.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.000
[Train horn]
[Goose honk]

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:36.140
It's what they were all waiting for, Gary.

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:38.260
That's what you paid your money for. I know that.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:40.640
What was the Standard Time Company selling?

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.820
How did they get their time to people?

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:46.780
I realize I'm treating time as a physical thing here.
How did they tell people the time?

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:48.480
MATT: Telephoning?

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:49.260
Nah.

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:53.260
Or telegramming? Or tele... communicating
in some kind of way on a wire?

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:55.440
TOM: ...there's a word. There's a word!

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:57.320
Telegraph! Morse code!

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:58.240
Text message!

00:14:58.420 --> 00:14:59.100
TOM: [Strangled noise]

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:00.100
MATT: Skype!

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:03.120
[DING] Gary gets the point. It was a
'telegraphic time signal service',

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:05.540
But I'm giving Matt a point as well,
[DING] because he was *so* close.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:06.760
GARY: Bulls***!

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.440
He got the idea, you got the word.

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:12.140
GARY: In mill towns, in t' north where we come from,

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:14.880
they'd go round, basically a man with a great big stick,

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.100
would just whack on the window to wake you up,

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:19.360
and that was the knocker-upper,
because he was the only one with a watch.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:21.320
Yeah. He was the knock-her-upper.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:22.360
[Laughter]

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:25.600
Lots of kids that looked suspiciously similar,
if you know what I mean.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:29.700
Yes, the Standard Time Company
sold a telegraphic time signal,

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:32.620
but by that point, we had radio, we had military time...

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:36.780
and the customers were starting to die out, essentially.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:38.480
Did you have the speaking clock by then as well?

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:41.920
19...36, so it did exist.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.320
There is one other signal at
the Royal Observatory in Greenwich...

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.920
Obviously she went up, got it from the clock,
got it from the official source.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:52.280
What happens every day at the
Royal Observatory on the top of the hill?

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:55.380
Someone gets their fingers trapped in a door
and yells, on the hour.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:57.860
No one's ever got round to fixing it.

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:02.400
GARY: 'I'll just get the milk — ' BOOM.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.420
GARY: 'AAGH! JESUS WEPT!'

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:05.860
Set the clock...

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:07.780
TOM: Where did that come from?!

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.360
Where does it all come from, Tom? I don't know.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:13.860
No... 1 pm at the top of the hill in
Greenwich Park, a thing happens.

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:15.480
GARY: Somebody drops the balls!

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:16.760
Point! [DING]

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:18.380
[Tittering]
TOM: Do you want to explain that?

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:20.660
That sounded worse than in my head... yeah.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.540
S***. Yeah. There's the time... ball, isn't there, that falls —

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:29.740
TOM: See, this is one of those things
that sounds rubbish, but actually isn't.

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:31.120
Thank you.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:32.760
TOM: That's actually a good mime.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.720
That's Lord Time himself there.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:36.540
Do you want to explain the time ball?

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:37.280
MATT: Please explain.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:40.980
I can't understand the time ball! It's a ball
on a stick that drops down at one o'clock.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.720
Is it something like so ships on the River Thames

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:45.940
could synchronize their chronometers
before they set off,

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.200
on a sail, so they knew it was one o'clock?

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.920
A bit like the one o'clock gun in Edinburgh, where
they fire a big f***-off gun at the top of the castle,

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.900
to scare all the tourists, 'cos that's
what it's for these days, let's face it.

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:57.600
I was once at Edinburgh Castle...

00:16:57.780 --> 00:16:58.760
Hold on.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:16:59.540
[DING DING]

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:00.360
Thank you!

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:01.740
TOM: Carry on.
GARY: Thank you.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:03.340
There's a third one needed.

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:05.860
I was once at Edinburgh Castle on holiday.

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:08.220
I was looking down through the telescope
as the one o'clock gun went off —

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:10.740
You see them bastards duck, down on Princes Street!

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:14.720
If you're ever in Vienna —

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:17.460
GARY: That means nothing to me.
MATT: [Sings] 'Oh, Vienna!'

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:23.340
They test the air raid sirens
in the city once a month still.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:24.980
GARY: Ho ho!
CHRIS: Oooh.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:28.580
Which I imagine is just to annoy tourists now.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:29.600
GARY: Yeah.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.260
Like, it's the three-minute warning,
the bomb's about to drop...

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.760
I want a helmet and to be able to run.

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:36.100
[Laughter]

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:39.360
Why are both those things unachievable for you?

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:49.780
Congratulations Chris, you win this one!

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:56.100
You win a gambling den for nocturnal birds of prey,

00:17:56.100 --> 00:18:00.020
run by the star of the Godfather
from the back of a '59 Chevy.

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:01.520
GARY: Oh, Jesus Christ, no.

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.020
Someone just got that, I think.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:05.880
It's Al Pacino's El Camino Owl Casino.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.780
With that, we say thank you to... Chris Joel!

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:17.440
To Gary Brannan!

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:19.600
Matt Gray!

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:21.000
Bye-bye, studio audience!

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:22.980
We will see you in ten minutes after the interval!

